True; animals have been known to attack humans.  Generally, when this
happens, the animal is said to have "gone wild" and will often be put
to sleep (killed).  In NH, pets "go wild" if you leave them alone too
long, starve them, etc.; it's typically referred to as "going feral",
at which point it is no longer a pet and probably gets killed by its
(former) owner.  My issue is that NH pets will *always* act like rabid
little beasties which are "yours" in so far as they follow you around,
looking for things to kill.  Not the sort of behavior one generally
expects from a "pet".

As for your second point(s)--people have been known to play without
stealing from shops, especially if they're neutral/lawful, and direct
theft causes the game to call the Kops, similar to RL.  As for
"gratuitously laying into every newt," etc., true, we don't accept
it--but self defence is perfectly allowed.  If a tiger attacks me, and
I have a gun, I am well in my rights to shoot it in order to save my
life.  In NH, the vast majority of the monsters are hostile, and thus
you could argue that those combats are "self defence".  If I shoot a
peaceful tiger, however, I'm most likely to end up in jail; NH
punishes the player for directly killing a peaceful critter, but not
for allowing their pet to do so.  Your analogy seems to fail, unless
I'm missing something.

